Subject: [PATCH] easystick: riscv: esp32p4: return from signal handlers through the kernel's sigreturn code (#44)

Without an MMU there is no vDSO, so setup_rt_frame() copies the two
instructions that call rt_sigreturn (li a7, __NR_rt_sigreturn; ecall) into
the signal frame and makes them visible to instruction fetch with
flush_icache_range().  That is flush_icache_all(), which with more than
one CPU online sends an IPI to every other CPU and waits for it
(arch/riscv/mm/cacheflush.c).  With both CPUs online, a program that
returned from a SIGSEGV handler 20,000 times (40,000 faults) took 2.69 s
on CPU0 and 2.50 s on CPU1, against 1.49 s on the 1-CPU build.

User code runs in M-mode on this SoC (0031), and the kernel image is in
the same PSRAM window (0x48000000) that user programs run from, so a
handler can return through the kernel's own copy, __user_rt_sigreturn in
.rodata.  Point ra there: no copy and no flush.  The frame keeps its
sigreturn_code slot, so its layout does not change.

---
--- a/arch/riscv/kernel/signal.c
+++ b/arch/riscv/kernel/signal.c
@@ -463,6 +463,15 @@
 	if (is_shstk_enabled(current))
 		regs->t0 = regs->ra;
 
+#elif defined(CONFIG_SOC_ESP32P4)
+	/*
+	 * User code runs in M-mode here and can run the kernel's own copy of
+	 * the two instructions.  Copying them to the stack needs
+	 * flush_icache_range(), which is flush_icache_all(): with two CPUs
+	 * online, an IPI to the other one and a wait for it, which made a
+	 * handled signal on two CPUs almost twice as slow as on one.
+	 */
+	regs->ra = (unsigned long)__user_rt_sigreturn;
 #else
 	/*
 	 * For the nommu case we don't have a VDSO.  Instead we push two
